        }}
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="report-container">
        <h1>BIEK Computer Science 12th Class Past Paper Analysis (2015–2025)</h1>
        <p class="timestamp">Generated: {datetime.now().strftime('%B %d, %Y')} | Target Audience: Educators & Curriculum Planners</p>

        <h2>Dataset & Examination Overview</h2>
        <p>This report provides a comprehensive trend analysis of question types, frequency patterns, and topic distribution across 11 years (2015-2025) of BIEK Computer Science HSC Part-2 examination papers. It is designed to enable educators, curriculum planners, and students to understand exam evolution and prepare effectively.</p>

        <div class="metric-card-container">
            <div class="metric-card">
                <div class="metric-label">Total Questions</div>
                <div class="metric-value">{total_questions}</div>
            </div>
            <div class="metric-card">
                <div class="metric-label">Years Analyzed</div>
                <div class="metric-value">{total_years}</div>
            </div>
            <div class="metric-card">
                <div class="metric-label">Most Common Type</div>
                <div class="metric-value">{type_counts.idxmax()} ({type_ratios.max()}%)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="metric-card">
                <div class="metric-label">Average Qs per Year</div>
                <div class="metric-value">{total_questions / total_years:.1f}</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h2>Year-by-Year Question Volume Analysis</h2>
        <p>The following chart visualizes the total number of questions appearing in each examination year. Understanding the yearly volume helps identify any structural shifts in the exam format.</p>

        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_yearly_dist}" alt="Yearly Distribution Chart">
        </div>

        <h3>Year-Over-Year Statistics</h3>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Year</th>
                    <th>Total Questions</th>
                    <th>YoY Change (Absolute)</th>
                    <th>YoY Change (%)</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
"""

for year in years:
    count = yearly_counts[year]
    diff = yoy_change[year] if not np.isnan(yoy_change[year]) else "N/A"
    pct = yoy_pct_change[year] if not np.isnan(yoy_pct_change[year]) else "N/A"

    diff_str = f"{diff:+.0f}" if isinstance(diff, (int, float)) and diff != "N/A" else "N/A"
    pct_str = f"{pct:+.1f}%" if isinstance(pct, (int, float)) and pct != "N/A" else "N/A"

    html_content += f"""
                <tr>
                    <td>{year}</td>
                    <td>{count}</td>
                    <td>{diff_str}</td>
                    <td>{pct_str}</td>
                </tr>
    """

html_content += f"""
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>Question Type Composition & Trends</h2>
        <p>The exam comprises three main types of questions: MCQs, Short Questions, and Long Questions. The charts below illustrate how these proportions have shifted over the last decade.</p>

        <div style="display: flex; gap: 20px; flex-wrap: wrap;">
            <div class="chart-container" style="flex: 1; min-width: 400px;">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_type_ratio}" alt="Question Type Ratio Chart">
            </div>
            <div class="chart-container" style="flex: 1; min-width: 300px;">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_type_pie}" alt="Overall Question Type Pie Chart">
            </div>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Baseline Averages:</strong></p>
        <ul>
"""

for q_type, avg in avg_per_type.items():
    html_content += f"<li>{q_type}: {avg:.1f} questions per year</li>"

html_content += f"""
        </ul>

        <h2>Recurring Topics & Question Content Patterns</h2>
        <p>By analyzing the frequency of keywords extracted from the question text (excluding generic stop words and MCQ options), we can identify the core topics that form the backbone of the BIEK Computer Science curriculum.</p>

        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart_keywords}" alt="Keyword Frequency Chart">
        </div>

        <p><strong>Top 5 Core Concepts Tested:</strong></p>
        <ol>
"""
for w, c in word_freq[:5]:
    html_content += f"<li><strong>{w.title()}</strong> ({c} occurrences)</li>"

html_content += f"""
        </ol>

        <h2>Strategic Insights for Exam Preparation</h2>
        <div class="insights-box">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Consistent Focus Areas:</strong> Based on the keyword analysis, topics related to <em>{word_freq[0][0]}, {word_freq[1][0]},</em> and <em>{word_freq[2][0]}</em> appear with high regularity. Educators should ensure these foundational concepts are heavily emphasized in revision plans.</li>
                <li><strong>Format Shifts:</strong> The year-over-year question volume and composition charts indicate whether the exam leans more towards objective (MCQ) testing versus subjective (Short/Long) evaluations in recent years. Adjust mock exam formats to mirror the most recent 3-4 years of data.</li>
                <li><strong>Study Strategy:</strong> Because MCQs comprise {type_ratios.get('MCQs', 0)}% of the total historical dataset, students must maintain a strong grasp of discrete facts and syntax rules. Conversely, Long questions (accounting for {type_ratios.get('Long', 0)}%) necessitate deep conceptual understanding and structured answer writing.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>Conclusions & Limitations</h2>
